      "title": "Cost-of-living adjustment",
      "narration": "Rosa’s 3,600 dollar benefit is fixed. If she is disabled for twenty years, inflation could cut its buying power in half. A cost-of-living adjustment rider increases the benefit during a claim, usually after the first twelve months of disability, by a fixed percentage or by the change in the consumer price index, often with a cap. It does not raise the benefit before a claim begins. The rider is one of the more expensive additions because long claims are exactly the ones it grows. For a 41-year-old with benefits to 65, Theo recommends it.",
